{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "איש אשר ינאף", :translit "ish asher yinaf", :gloss "a man who commits adultery"} {:he "מקלל אביו ואמו", :translit "mekalel aviv v'imo", :gloss "curses his father and his mother"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "juxtaposition (semikhut) of adjacent Torah sections: adulterer passage near parent-curse passage"}], :frame {:text "The Torah places the prohibition against adultery (Leviticus 20:10) in close proximity to the prohibition against cursing one's parents (Leviticus 20:9). The Ba'al HaTurim reads this juxtaposition thematically: the mamzer born of an adulterous union lacks certain knowledge of his father's identity, which leads him to curse the man he suspects is his father. The laws of the mamzer, who may not marry into the congregation, are stated at Deuteronomy 23:3. The prohibition against a son cursing his father appears at Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.", :sources ["Leviticus 20:9" "Leviticus 20:10" "Deuteronomy 23:3" "Exodus 21:17"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "juxtaposition (semikhut) of adjacent Torah sections: adulterer passage near parent-curse passage", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:24:37.171246223Z", :model "glm-5.2"}